  ],
  "text": "Saturn is raining its own rings into itself, a swimming pool of ice every half hour. Every picture you have ever seen of Saturn has those rings in it. But the rings are not rock at all. They are almost pure water ice. And Saturn's magnetic field grabs the smallest charged grains and pulls them in. So that ice slides down the field lines and falls as rain into the clouds. At that rate the whole ring system drains in under a hundred million years. The stranger part is that the rings are newer than the last dinosaurs. Saturn spent almost all of its four and a half billion years with nothing around it. You are alive in the thin slice of time when Saturn has rings. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
